---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const stiffnessCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '200*x', label: 'steel (E ≈ 200 GPa)', color: '#475569', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '70*x', label: 'aluminium (E ≈ 70 GPa)', color: '#d97706' },
    { expr: '11*x', label: 'wood, along grain (E ≈ 11 GPa)', color: '#4d7c0f' },
    { expr: '1500', label: 'typical yield line', color: 'rgba(180,35,24,0.6)', width: 1.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 0.02, label: 'strain (fraction)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 4.5, label: 'stress (GPa)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 360,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/foundational/why-materials-behave">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Materials science is one idea applied relentlessly: <strong>structure determines
    properties</strong>. How atoms bond and pack decides whether a material bends, shatters,
    conducts or insulates. The first property every engineer needs is <strong>stiffness</strong> —
    how much a material stretches under load — measured by Young's modulus, E: the slope of
    stress against strain in the elastic region.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="three stiffness slopes, one failure line — where each crosses is roughly where that material stops bouncing back" widget="plotter" config={stiffnessCfg} />

  <p>
    Steel is ~200 GPa, aluminium ~70, wood along the grain ~11 — an 18× spread explained purely
    by atomic bonding strength. Where each line meets the yield region, elastic stretching gives
    way to permanent deformation. That crossing point is the engineer's first safety calculation.
  </p>

  <h2>The four families</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Metals</strong> — metallic bonding gives the electron sea: stiff, ductile, conductive. Bend a paperclip: it yields plastically.</li>
    <li><strong>Ceramics</strong> — ionic/covalent bonds: very stiff and hard, but brittle. No electron sea, no bending — cracks propagate instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Polymers</strong> — long coiled molecules: floppy, light, temperature-sensitive (think spaghetti vs frozen spaghetti).</li>
    <li><strong>Composites</strong> — designed combinations: glass fibre in resin, carbon in bikes. Each material contributes what the other lacks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How the field thinks</h2>
  <p>
    Every property question becomes a structure question. Why is diamond hard and graphite soft
    when both are pure carbon? Different crystal structures — 3D tetrahedral network vs slippery
    2D sheets. That causal chain — <em>processing → structure → properties → performance</em> —
    is the discipline's spine, and it makes materials design an engineering science rather than
    trial and error.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ashby, Materials Selection in Mechanical Design; Callister & Rethwisch, Materials Science and Engineering.</p>
</TopicPage>
